<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hieronymus Bosch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1450–<a href="/years/1516">1516</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Jheronimus van Aken, Jheronimus Bosch, Bosch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hieronymus Bosch, born Jheronimus van Aken, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> painter from Brabant and one of the most notable representatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-netherlandish-painting">Early Netherlandish painting</a> school. He spent most of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/s-hertogenbosch">'s-Hertogenbosch</a>, which gave him his surname. His works, generally oil on oak wood, mainly illustrate religious concepts and narratives, with macabre depictions of hell, and were collected in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in his lifetime. Only about 25 paintings and eight drawings are confidently identified as his; his most acclaimed works are three triptych altarpieces, particularly The Garden of Earthly Delights. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pieter-bruegel-the-elder">Pieter Bruegel the Elder</a> was his best-known follower.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hieronymus-bosch.jpg" alt="Hieronymus Bosch" width="330" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Jacques Le Boucq · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jheronimus_Bosch_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
